<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portus Itius</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portus Itius was the harbour in the land of the Morini, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> sailed for his second invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 54 BCE. Caesar describes it as the most convenient crossing point, about 30 Roman miles from Britain. Its location is uncertain: most scholars identify it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, the later Roman port of Gesoriacum, while others have proposed Wissant or other sites on the coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portus-itius.jpg" alt="Portus Itius" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mandrak; derivative work by Cristiano64 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_harbors_and_fleets_Augustus-Severus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The port lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> in what is now Nord-Pas-de-Calais, near the uplands round Cap Gris Nez, the Promunturium Itium. Caesar names it twice, as the place where he called his ships together to embark troops in 54 BCE. Its exact site has been disputed since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and Saint-Omer (Sithiu), Wissant and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, the Roman Gesoriacum and later Bononia, have all been proposed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Caesar does not name the port in his account of the first expedition, which has led some to think the two crossings started from different places, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions Itium in connection with the first. It was once generally agreed that he first sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>. In the Classical Review of May 1909, T. Rice Holmes argued for Wissant, 4 miles east of Gris Nez, for the second crossing: Caesar had found he could not launch even eighty ships at once from the small harbour of Boulogne, and the second expedition had more than 800, which could have been hauled up the beach and launched together at Wissant.</p><p>In 1944, against the backdrop of the Allied invasion of Normandy, the French historian Albert Grenier read Caesar's text again against what was known of the Channel's wind, weather, tides and silting, and concluded that the departure ports were likely at Saint-Omer. In Caesar's day a large lagoon lay behind a fringe of coastal islands where Calais and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> now stand, and an estuary reached inland to the site of Saint-Omer; it has since become farmland.</p><p>Later crossings are linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>: it is presumed to have been the starting point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>'s abortive invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> around 40 CE, where a Roman lighthouse believed to be his once stood, and of the conquest of 43 CE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a>. Suetonius records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> sailed from Gesoriacum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Itius Portus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caesar campaigned against them in 56 and 55 BCE and sailed for Britain from <b>Portus Itius</b> in their land, probably Boulogne.</blockquote><a href="/state/morini">Morini</a></li></ul></section>
